    <mj-text
      padding="10px 12px"
      font-family="Austin News Text Web, TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif"
      color="#000000"
    >
      <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 400; margin: 0;">
        Dubai tightens building rules
      </h2>
    </mj-text>
    <mj-image
          border-radius="10px"
          padding="10px 12px"
          width="600px"
          src="https://www.dubaisummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
          alt="REPLACE_ME"
          href="https://www.dubaisummary.com/"
          target="_blank"
        />

    <mj-text padding="10px 12px 16px 12px" font-family="Arial" color="#000000">
      <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What happened?</strong> Sheikh Mohammed issued Law No. (3) of 2026 to strengthen building quality, safety, and maintenance standards across the emirate. <a href="https://www.mepmiddleeast.com/news/dubai-announces-law-no-3-of-2026-on-building-quality-and-safety#:~:text=The%20law%20introduces%20a%20Quality,procedures%20set%20by%20the%20authorities." target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #102341; color: black;">The law applies to all buildings in Dubai</a>, including those in private developments and free zones such as DIFC, whether they were built before or after the law’s introduction. It gives Dubai Municipality a central role in oversight, including creating a digital management system, maintaining a unified building database, carrying out inspections, and enforcing standards.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What changes?</strong> Building owners must now obtain a Quality and Safety Certificate after completion, based on an inspection by a licensed engineering office. Owners are also required to fix identified defects, carry out periodic maintenance, and allow official inspections. Certificates will remain valid for 10 years for buildings under 40 years old and 5 years for older buildings, with renewals possible under future executive rules.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> The law is designed to improve structural safety, reduce accidents, protect lives and property, and preserve Dubai’s urban quality. Violations can trigger fines from Dh100 to Dh1 million, rising to Dh2 million for repeat offences, along with permit suspensions and other enforcement measures.</p>
    </mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
<mj-spacer height="10px" />
